"Internal Error ()\nciteturn1071view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22 is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn1071view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22.md is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn1071view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22/transcript.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22/transcript.md is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn1071view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22.json\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://aidailybrief.ai/e/2026-09-22.json is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew ClosedQuorum Windows malware uses AI for attack decisions (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-closedquorum-windows-malware-uses-ai-for-attack-decisions/)\nciteturn1071view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1069view6\",\"id\":0}); Total lines: 118\nL0: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input: Search Site]\nL1: \nL2: [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: # New ClosedQuorum Windows malware uses AI for attack decisions\nL5: \nL6: By\nL7: ###### cite0†Bill Toulas L8: \nL9:   * September 22, 2026\nL10:   * 02:04 PM\nL11:   * cite1†0 L12: \nL13: cite2†Image: New ClosedQuorum Windows malware uses AI for attack decisions†www.bleepstatic.com L14: \nL15: A new Windows malware named ClosedQuorum uses Google Gemini, DeepSeek, Qwen, and Mistral AI models to autonomously determine the actions to take during post-compromise stages of an attack.\nL16: The Go-based malware acts with no commands from a human operator, using reconnaissance information and a voting system to decide its next step on infected hosts.\nL17: \nL18: When votes are tied, DeepSeek has priority at making the final call, based on the option it considers most appropriate, followed by Qwen, Mistral, and Gemini.\nL19: \nL20: Cisco Talos researchers analyzing ClosedQuorum say that the models are restricted to a predefined set of decisions, including:\nL21:   * steal — simultaneously runs LSASS credential dumping, browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox) credential theft, and cryptocurrency-wallet (MetaMast, Exodus, Ethereum) extraction\nL22:   * inject — generates shellcode, then uses either process hollowing or Early Bird APC injection\nL23:   * persist — executes the malware’s persistence module\nL24:   * move — included as a possible lateral movement decision, but the analyzed distribution build has no corresponding handler, so it cannot execute this action\nL25: The stolen details are then passed to the operators via a Discord webhook, so apart from the malware delivery, the attack can be fully automated.\nL26: \nL27: cite3†Image: ClosedQuorum attack overview†www.bleepstatic.com ClosedQuorum attack model overview\nL28: Source: Cisco\nL29: \nL30: Talos describes ClosedQuorum as the first publicly documented Windows implant to delegate tactical command-and-control (C2) decisions to a panel of AI models, commenting that this adds greater speed and scaling potential to malicious operations.\nL31: The researchers also highlight that this eliminates human interaction, allowing the attack chain to proceed at any time.\nL32: \nL33: However, Cisco notes that this may also pose challenges in certain cases where rate limits are hit, output is malformed, or commercial APIs that the system relies on are temporarily unavailable.\nL34: Since it is not a sophisticated piece of malware, it is unclear if ClosedQuorum is a test or experiment. However, the researchers warn that it represents an \"architectural shift towards attack-chain automation.\"\nL35: \nL36: “While we do not have confirmation of in-the-wild deployment, artifacts from the binary were used to connect the developer to postings on criminal forums related to carding, dating back to 2025,” cite4†explains Cisco Talos†blog.talosintelligence.com .\nL37: The binary that Cisco Talos analyzed contains placeholder API credentials and a dummy Discord webhook, although the creators can add those in their custom ClosedQuorum builds.\nL38: \nL39: Cisco Talos discovered ClosedQuorum through cite5†CAIRN†blog.talosintelligence.com , an cite6†open-source toolkit†github.com it released to help researchers track and analyze AI-integrated malware.\nL40: ## cite7†Build your security blueprint for AI-powered attacks†hubs.li L41: \nL42: Join Mikko Hyppönen and security leaders from the NFL, CHANEL, and Atlassian for a two-hour digital summit on what AI-speed attacks change, what defenders should stop doing, and how to validate, decide, fix, and re-validate at machine speed.\nL43: \nL44: cite7†Save your seat†hubs.li L45: \nL46:   * cite8†AI L47:   * cite9†AI Malware L48:   * cite10†Artificial Intelligence L49:   * cite11†ClosedQuorum L50:   * cite12†Malware L51:   *   *   *   *   * ##### cite0†Bill Toulas bill.toulas@bleepingcomputer.com\nL52: \nL53: Bill Toulas is a tech writer and infosec news reporter with over a decade of experience working on various online publications, covering open-source, Linux, malware, data breach incidents, and hacks.\nL54: \nL55:   * cite13†Previous Article L56:   * cite14†Next Article L57: ##### Post a Comment cite15†Community Rules L58: ###### You need to login in order to post a comment\nL